<stack-static>
    <statement><p><p>Each matrix below represents one transformation. Choose its name.</p><p>Hint: where do (1, 0) and (0, 1) go?</p><div>(a) <m>{\left[\begin{array}{cc} -1 &amp; 0 \\ 0 &amp; -1 \end{array}\right]}</m> [ the identity (no change) | reflection in the x-axis | reflection in the y-axis | reflection in the line y = x | reflection in the line y = −x | rotation of 90° anticlockwise about O | rotation of 180° about O | rotation of 90° clockwise about O ]  </div><div>(b) <m>{\left[\begin{array}{cc} -1 &amp; 0 \\ 0 &amp; 1 \end{array}\right]}</m> [ the identity (no change) | reflection in the x-axis | reflection in the y-axis | reflection in the line y = x | reflection in the line y = −x | rotation of 90° anticlockwise about O | rotation of 180° about O | rotation of 90° clockwise about O ]  </div><div>(c) <m>{\left[\begin{array}{cc} 1 &amp; 0 \\ 0 &amp; -1 \end{array}\right]}</m> [ the identity (no change) | reflection in the x-axis | reflection in the y-axis | reflection in the line y = x | reflection in the line y = −x | rotation of 90° anticlockwise about O | rotation of 180° about O | rotation of 90° clockwise about O ]  </div><div>(d) <m>{\left[\begin{array}{cc} 0 &amp; 1 \\ -1 &amp; 0 \end{array}\right]}</m> [ the identity (no change) | reflection in the x-axis | reflection in the y-axis | reflection in the line y = x | reflection in the line y = −x | rotation of 90° anticlockwise about O | rotation of 180° about O | rotation of 90° clockwise about O ]  </div></p></statement>
    <solution><p><p>Read the columns: the first column of a matrix is where (1, 0) goes and the second is where (0, 1) goes. Sketch the unit square and its image, then name the transformation.</p><ul><li>(a) <m>(1,\,0)</m> goes to <m>(-1,\,0)</m> and <m>(0,\,1)</m> goes to <m>(0,\,-1)</m>: both coordinates change sign but do not swap, <m>(x,\,y)</m> to <m>(-x,\,-y)</m>. That is a half turn about O.</li><li>(b) <m>(1,\,0)</m> goes to <m>(-1,\,0)</m> and <m>(0,\,1)</m> stays where it is: only the x-coordinate changes sign, so points flip across the y-axis. It is the reflection in the y-axis.</li><li>(c) <m>(1,\,0)</m> stays where it is and <m>(0,\,1)</m> goes to <m>(0,\,-1)</m>: only the y-coordinate changes sign, so points flip across the x-axis. It is the reflection in the x-axis.</li><li>(d) <m>(1,\,0)</m> goes to <m>(0,\,-1)</m> and <m>(0,\,1)</m> goes to <m>(1,\,0)</m>: a quarter turn clockwise about O.</li></ul></p></solution>
    <answer><p>rotation of 180° about O</p></answer>
    <answer><p>reflection in the y-axis</p></answer>
    <answer><p>reflection in the x-axis</p></answer>
    <answer><p>rotation of 90° clockwise about O</p></answer>
</stack-static>